1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,640
Hello everybody, welcome to digging for XSS Gold, unearthing browser quirks with Shazza.

2
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:20,780
Shazza is a fuzzing tool built on a simple but powerful principle.

3
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:24,040
Don't look for bugs, look for differences.

4
00:00:24,860 --> 00:00:27,200
Use the differences to find bugs.

5
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:35,440
Shazza helps you reveal security vulnerabilities by analysing deviations and unexpected behaviours.

6
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:45,580
So what exactly is fuzzing?

7
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:53,360
Well, fuzzing automates the process of asking millions of questions and receiving millions of answers.

8
00:00:53,360 --> 00:01:01,540
Fuzzing typically involves generating a large number of inputs, either randomly or systematically,

9
00:01:01,980 --> 00:01:09,180
and applying them to a target program, template, or system to detect unexpected behaviours or vulnerabilities.

10
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:22,600
Shazza is a tool designed to streamline the fuzzing process for web browsers by automating several tedious tasks.

11
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:31,300
Fuzzing typically involves generating loops, replacing data in templates, and storing results for different browsers.

12
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:41,680
Shazza simplifies this by handling browser detection, data storage, template generation, and placeholder insertion.

13
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:51,400
The name Shazza is derived from combining shared and fuzzer, reflecting on its goal to make fuzzing easier and shareable.

14
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:58,540
So now we know what Shazza is, let's focus on the fuzzing types.

15
00:01:59,060 --> 00:02:01,160
What are the fuzzing types in Shazza?

16
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:08,960
Shazza has three fuzzing types, HTML, JS, and XSS.

17
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:14,480
The HTML type is used to understand how HTML is parsed.

18
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:23,600
For instance, if you need to determine what character can close a HTML comment, you would use the HTML fuz type.

19
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:28,640
It's more suitable when JavaScript execution is not required.

20
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:34,340
The JS type is used when JavaScript execution needs to be analysed.

21
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:41,000
The XSS type is needed when you need a combination of HTML and JavaScript.

22
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:48,000
One good example of this is when you need to know what characters are allowed before a HTML attribute.

23
00:02:51,080 --> 00:03:00,040
The first example is a HTML fuzze template with placeholders for a character and the found tag.

24
00:03:00,620 --> 00:03:07,900
Since the HTML fuzze type doesn't support JavaScript, the found tag is used to log the tag results.

25
00:03:07,900 --> 00:03:16,980
If the fuzze character is greater than, for example, it will close the HTML comment, which exposes the found tag.

26
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:22,660
This specter helps you identify characters which close a HTML comment.

27
00:03:22,660 --> 00:03:30,760
The second JavaScript example, we want to know what characters are allowed before an identifier.

28
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:35,380
The first placeholder is the character to fuzz.

29
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:40,020
And the second one is the iteration number of the fuzz or the character code.

30
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:48,780
The last example shows how you can check what characters are allowed before an on error event.

31
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:51,740
And those are the three fuzzing types in Shazza.

32
00:03:55,820 --> 00:04:00,160
When I approach fuzzing, there's usually two approaches that I take.

33
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:03,360
It's useful to have a question in mind.

34
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:11,680
So, for example, what characters are allowed before the greater than that will still close the HTML comment?

35
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:18,180
Once you have that question in mind, you can tailor the template to answer that question.

36
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:23,320
Another way is to fuzz for existing behavior.

37
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:31,760
Let's imagine that the browser silently adds support for a new attribute quote using backticks.

38
00:04:31,760 --> 00:04:37,280
You can find this behavior by fuzzing for the existing quote behavior.

39
00:04:37,960 --> 00:04:45,100
So, the browser might accidentally add support for this character or it might be unspecked.

40
00:04:48,020 --> 00:04:53,100
In this example, we place the placeholder before the greater than character.

41
00:04:54,100 --> 00:05:00,620
And if this comment is successfully closed, the found tag will be found and it will log the results.

42
00:05:00,620 --> 00:05:04,620
You might be thinking, well, this isn't going to happen.

43
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:07,020
But actually, it did happen on Firefox.

44
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:14,660
And I found multiple bugs where you could use new lines and it will still close the HTML comment using this trick.

45
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:22,620
In this example, I create a title attribute with two character placeholders.

46
00:05:22,620 --> 00:05:30,560
Only if the characters act as an attribute quote will the on error event fire.

47
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:39,460
When running this vector, the single quotes and double quotes will be logged, confirming this quote behavior.

48
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:45,660
But hypothetically, if a browser introduced a new character that behaved in the same way,

49
00:05:46,020 --> 00:05:48,040
you would see results for that browser.

50
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:57,580
So, if a browser accidentally added a certain character, you would see the results for those characters.

51
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:00,000
So, this is a new approach of fuzzing.

52
00:06:00,420 --> 00:06:04,360
You fuzz for existing behavior as well as answering questions.

53
00:06:04,360 --> 00:06:13,600
So, now we've covered the basics of Shazza, let's look at finding interesting browser behavior.

54
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:19,860
So, just a quick recap on URL credentials.

55
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:29,940
A URL can include credentials where the username and password are separated by a column and followed by an art symbol.

56
00:06:29,940 --> 00:06:33,860
In Chrome, this is completely hidden from the user.

57
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:36,580
So, the credentials are there, but you can't see them.

58
00:06:37,980 --> 00:06:47,620
And you can access these credentials client-side by using document.url, document.baseURI, or the URL constructor.

59
00:06:47,620 --> 00:06:59,520
So, I recently researched hiding payloads in the credentials part of a URL by testing characters that don't URL encode.

60
00:07:00,060 --> 00:07:09,960
So, to create a template, I first created an anchor tag, assigned a URL, and then fuzzed the username with different characters.

61
00:07:10,620 --> 00:07:15,900
Then, I checked if the browser encoded them, revealing which characters are allowed.

62
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:20,600
So, in this example, I use code before the fuzz.

63
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:21,820
So, this creates the element.

64
00:07:22,380 --> 00:07:25,520
And then, the template is used to do the actual fuzzing.

65
00:07:29,860 --> 00:07:38,820
Whilst researching DOM XSS, I aim to identify what characters' browsers don't URL encode in the URL.

66
00:07:38,820 --> 00:07:44,780
So, I used the DOM again, and I used a JavaScript fuzz type.

67
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:52,880
But this time, I assigned to the anchor hash, and then checked that property to see if it had been URL encoded by the browser.

68
00:07:53,680 --> 00:07:58,440
So, this would put up interesting results if the browser wasn't URL encoded.

69
00:07:58,440 --> 00:08:05,440
So, so far, we've covered fuzzing single characters.

70
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:07,820
But Shazza can do more than that.

71
00:08:08,520 --> 00:08:14,520
A template can also fuzz data, like HTML tags, attributes, entities.

72
00:08:15,320 --> 00:08:18,500
You basically define your own data that you want to fuzz.

73
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:20,580
Choose it from the select menu.

74
00:08:20,580 --> 00:08:24,960
So, you choose the data1 or data2 placeholder to fuzz.

75
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:31,180
And then, when you create that vector, you can use those placeholders to fuzz that data.

76
00:08:31,860 --> 00:08:33,760
So, you could be fuzzing attributes.

77
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:35,900
You could be fuzzing HTML tags.

78
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:37,520
You could be looking for crashes.

79
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:39,500
You could be looking for interesting behavior.

80
00:08:39,980 --> 00:08:44,840
But you can use the data in Shazza to enumerate on that data.

81
00:08:44,840 --> 00:08:52,640
In this vector, I create a div element and assign an anchor tag.

82
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:59,300
I then use the placeholder data1 to insert various entities for fuzzing.

83
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:04,660
So, after each iteration, I check the host property of the anchor.

84
00:09:05,180 --> 00:09:14,060
And if it differs from example.com, it will allow me to detect if the injected entity has altered the intended URL.

85
00:09:14,060 --> 00:09:19,060
So, I use the data1 placeholder to insert the entity.

86
00:09:19,060 --> 00:09:23,880
So, in the example, I use code before the fuzz to create the div.

87
00:09:24,480 --> 00:09:27,080
Then, I assign the anchor to the div.

88
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:31,100
And then, I use the data1 placeholder to insert the entity.

89
00:09:31,640 --> 00:09:36,920
And then, after the code, after each fuzz, I check the host property and then log the results.

90
00:09:36,920 --> 00:09:48,160
In this vector, I enumerate all entities, checking which ones convert to the greater man character.

91
00:09:49,160 --> 00:09:55,840
An iframe is created with an entity assigned via the data1 placeholder to the src doc.

92
00:09:55,840 --> 00:10:06,560
After each fuzz iteration, the src doc attribute is checked using the include function to determine if the character was decoded.

93
00:10:07,340 --> 00:10:15,140
This was cool because it would find you any entities that convert to a greater man.

94
00:10:15,140 --> 00:10:24,840
After you've fuzzed your vectors, you can view the vector on the vector overview page.

95
00:10:25,280 --> 00:10:27,700
So, let's check out the last vector results.

96
00:10:30,920 --> 00:10:35,780
Shazer will generate some sample payloads based on the data that it's found.

97
00:10:35,780 --> 00:10:41,520
So, in our fuzzing process, we targeted entities that convert to greater than.

98
00:10:42,020 --> 00:10:50,400
In this sample, you can see that both ampersandGT and ampersandNVGT meet this condition.

99
00:10:51,200 --> 00:10:58,700
So, this is just a sample of data, which Shazer will show you what entities or what data is being converted.

100
00:10:58,700 --> 00:10:59,700
Shazer will show you what entities are being converted to the data.

101
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:06,740
So, after the sample payloads, then we have the actual fuzz results for the browser.

102
00:11:07,460 --> 00:11:12,100
What Shazer does is only expand the browser results if they differ.

103
00:11:12,780 --> 00:11:18,520
So, it'll contract all the other browsers unless they have a different behavior than another one.

104
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:23,460
In this example, it shows the data in the form of entities.

105
00:11:23,460 --> 00:11:29,240
And you can sort the data by filtering out alphanumerics and show only the differences.

106
00:11:30,180 --> 00:11:38,960
So, I don't know if you can see, but in this example, I show ampersandGT, ampersandGT in uppercase, and ampersandNVGT.

107
00:11:39,320 --> 00:11:46,100
All these create the greater than character, which can be used for XSS on some obscure system.

108
00:11:46,100 --> 00:11:59,400
When fuzzing characters, Shazer will generate a table with the decimal number of each character, the hex representation of it, and the character itself.

109
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:05,700
In this sample, it's highlighted 39, which is the character code for a single quote.

110
00:12:05,700 --> 00:12:10,560
And this is because at least one browser had different behavior.

111
00:12:11,280 --> 00:12:16,580
So, it will highlight in red when one browser differs out of all the browsers that have been tested.

112
00:12:17,080 --> 00:12:20,700
And this is really useful for finding deviations and unexpected behavior.

113
00:12:20,700 --> 00:12:31,100
So, when building Shazer, I faced a bit of a challenge, sharing vectors with others.

114
00:12:31,920 --> 00:12:37,540
To make this possible, I needed to eliminate XSS risks on the Shazer domain.

115
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:43,180
Without this, a malicious vector could compromise somebody else's account.

116
00:12:43,740 --> 00:12:47,360
This next section covers how I tackled this problem.

117
00:12:50,700 --> 00:12:55,680
So, when designing Shazer, I followed a few core principles.

118
00:12:56,720 --> 00:13:04,380
First of all, all the data is public, eliminating private vectors that could be targeted for theft.

119
00:13:05,240 --> 00:13:08,040
Second, there's no private data stored.

120
00:13:08,520 --> 00:13:15,880
By removing all private information like email addresses, the system holds no sensitive information to steal.

121
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:24,740
Lastly, I chose GitHub as the SSO provider, ensuring there are no passwords to steal on the system at any point.

122
00:13:25,580 --> 00:13:37,380
So, by designing the system without any data that you can steal, even in a compromise, there'll be a lot less damage than a normal website.

123
00:13:37,380 --> 00:13:48,340
All vectors are executed within an iframe sandbox configured with allow scripts and allow form flags.

124
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:53,380
To enable scripts and forms will prevent an attacker from breaking out of the frame.

125
00:13:53,940 --> 00:13:59,620
The SRC dock attribute is used to assign the template and the fuzzing architecture,

126
00:14:00,280 --> 00:14:03,980
ensuring that everything runs securely within the sandbox environment.

127
00:14:03,980 --> 00:14:12,320
I also use CSP to prevent excessive server requests, such as laws that may occur with image resources.

128
00:14:13,540 --> 00:14:18,520
Once fuzzing is complete, a web message is sent to the parent page.

129
00:14:19,160 --> 00:14:27,200
The parent then validates this message by comparing the message source property with the iframe's content window.

130
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:36,380
After a valid message has been received, the SRC of the iframe is cleared, and then that will remove any residual content.

131
00:14:37,540 --> 00:14:43,740
So, the source property is really important here because if you use the origin, the origin will be null.

132
00:14:44,520 --> 00:14:52,660
So, by using the source property, you can verify the iframe's window is the expected window that you're expecting.

133
00:14:52,660 --> 00:14:57,200
So, here's how it works.

134
00:14:57,760 --> 00:15:00,000
The user presses the fuzz button.

135
00:15:01,340 --> 00:15:07,340
The template and architecture is assigned to the SRC dock attribute.

136
00:15:08,360 --> 00:15:11,720
The sandbox iframe is then rendered, showed here in red.

137
00:15:12,120 --> 00:15:15,820
It blocks access to cookies and the alerts and prompts and stuff.

138
00:15:15,820 --> 00:15:21,420
And then the fuzz results are then sent to the parent page.

139
00:15:23,160 --> 00:15:25,260
So, this is the main Shazam window.

140
00:15:25,460 --> 00:15:26,960
It receives that message.

141
00:15:28,320 --> 00:15:35,400
The parent page receives a message, validates it, comes from the sandbox iframe, and then the fuzz results are stored.

142
00:15:35,400 --> 00:15:44,980
So, all you do is you look at the web message, use the event source property, and compare it with the iframe content window.

143
00:15:45,340 --> 00:15:48,740
And this verifies that the message is genuine.

144
00:15:49,300 --> 00:15:57,720
Now, you might get a problem, what I encountered, where you've got an extension installed, and it'll send a message to the event listener.

145
00:15:57,720 --> 00:16:00,720
So, if you do this, then you won't get that problem.

146
00:16:01,040 --> 00:16:10,220
So, like, for example, Reacts dev tools, they will send messages to listeners on the page.

147
00:16:10,640 --> 00:16:13,480
And then by comparing the source property, you can remove that.

148
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:17,540
So, this is the main reason why I validate the messages.

149
00:16:17,800 --> 00:16:21,520
And obviously, it also validates malicious messages too.

150
00:16:21,520 --> 00:16:32,320
So, in this section, I'll explore some more advanced vectors and demonstrate how you can leverage advanced placeholders.

151
00:16:36,280 --> 00:16:44,620
So, this vector detects which tags consume HTML, and it could be used to bypass a HTML filter.

152
00:16:44,620 --> 00:16:51,000
I think Masato Kinogawa used this technique to get XSS on Google, for example.

153
00:16:51,520 --> 00:17:01,740
So, in this example, the found tag will only be detected if the fuzz tag consumes the HTML and the image is not rendered.

154
00:17:02,440 --> 00:17:05,920
So, I've given an example of the text area because everybody can understand that.

155
00:17:06,020 --> 00:17:06,720
It's quite simple.

156
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:19,220
You use a text area, and unless you get a closing text area, it will just treat the resulting text as text, not as HTML.

157
00:17:19,220 --> 00:17:26,840
So, you can use this with a text area, but it also works with other tags, such as no embed, no script, etc.

158
00:17:26,840 --> 00:17:31,440
So, you can use Shazer to detect this sort of behavior.

159
00:17:31,440 --> 00:17:38,540
And that's really important because finding this sort of behavior can lead to a filter bypass, XSS, etc.

160
00:17:39,440 --> 00:17:43,540
So, here, the two examples show the text area and the no embed tag.

161
00:17:43,920 --> 00:17:47,940
But I've fuzzed other tags, and there are many other tags that have this behavior.

162
00:17:47,940 --> 00:17:58,920
Another thing to look for when fuzzing is that some tags will prevent other tags from rendering.

163
00:17:59,740 --> 00:18:03,700
And you can find this behavior by using the not found placeholder.

164
00:18:04,480 --> 00:18:10,900
This will only log data if the not found tag isn't found, as the name suggests.

165
00:18:10,900 --> 00:18:17,580
So, in this example, the script will not execute because the style neutralizes it.

166
00:18:17,960 --> 00:18:21,220
This is really good for finding HTML parsing quirks.

167
00:18:21,220 --> 00:18:33,200
For example, you get different behavior if you use an SVG tag with style, and if you use XML with style, or if you use HTML with style.

168
00:18:33,200 --> 00:18:41,540
So, this is really interesting to find because it can lead to bypasses and unexpected parsing quirks.

169
00:18:45,840 --> 00:18:50,440
So, in HTML attributes, entities get decoded.

170
00:18:51,100 --> 00:18:56,640
Meaning, if you use the log function, the entity will be decoded before being passed to it.

171
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:58,320
So, this is a problem.

172
00:18:58,320 --> 00:19:05,600
So, to solve this, you need to use an advanced placeholder that escapes the entity before logging it.

173
